WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.301 --> 00:00:03.603
Bladesmiths, welcome
to the forge.

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:06.839
And welcome to our legendary
bladesmithing competition

00:00:06.939 --> 00:00:10.142
like none other
you've ever experienced.

00:00:10.175 --> 00:00:11.811
Bladesmiths, here in the forge,

00:00:11.911 --> 00:00:13.312
we draw our inspiration

00:00:13.446 --> 00:00:16.049
from history
and history's legends

00:00:16.115 --> 00:00:17.450
like King Arthur,

00:00:17.516 --> 00:00:20.253
<i> and his legendary
 sword in the stone,</i>

00:00:20.353 --> 00:00:21.487
<i> Excalibur.</i>

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:23.923
Well, here in our first round
of competition today,

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.592
you guys will be
choosing your fate

00:00:26.726 --> 00:00:28.961
by drawing your own swords

00:00:29.062 --> 00:00:30.430
from this stone.

00:00:35.101 --> 00:00:37.337
Etched on the side of each one
of these blades

00:00:37.437 --> 00:00:39.973
is a particular
Damascus pattern

00:00:40.073 --> 00:00:43.042 line:0%
that you will have to execute
in your final blade

00:00:43.076 --> 00:00:44.444 line:0%
by the end of this round.

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:46.346
I'm gonna call you up
one at a time

00:00:46.479 --> 00:00:47.012
in a random order.

00:00:47.146 --> 00:00:49.581
And you'll draw your sword

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:51.017
from this stone.

00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:53.752
Scott, you're up first.
Pick your poison, buddy.

00:00:53.919 --> 00:00:56.189
[adventurous music playing]

00:00:58.824 --> 00:01:00.326
It's not the one, huh?

00:01:01.895 --> 00:01:03.463
Oh, there it is.

00:01:05.765 --> 00:01:09.034
-What do you got there, Scott?
-100 layer Damascus.

00:01:09.134 --> 00:01:10.804
Ooh, good luck
with that one, buddy.

00:01:10.904 --> 00:01:13.339
All right, Jarrett,
choose your sword.

00:01:18.277 --> 00:01:20.747
-What'd you pull?
-Ladder pattern Damascus.

00:01:21.981 --> 00:01:24.217
Colton, come
and choose your sword.

00:01:36.161 --> 00:01:37.663
All right, what'd
you pull, Colton?

00:01:37.797 --> 00:01:39.198
[Colton] Twisted Damascus.

00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:42.635
<i> How am I supposed to get
 a twisted Damascus blade</i>

00:01:42.735 --> 00:01:44.637
forged in three hours?

00:01:44.704 --> 00:01:46.872
Your three hours starts now.

00:01:46.972 --> 00:01:49.709
[Scott]<i> I choose 16 layers</i>

00:01:49.809 --> 00:01:52.312
alternating of 1095
and 15N20.

00:01:52.478 --> 00:01:54.546
<i> My plan is to get
 enough small pieces</i>

00:01:54.647 --> 00:01:57.516
that I can stack together
to form my 100 layers.

00:01:58.183 --> 00:02:00.519
It's too frickin' big
for this forge.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:04.023
This is easily the hardest thing
I've ever done.

00:02:04.123 --> 00:02:05.858
[Wil] Colton's 18 years old.

00:02:05.992 --> 00:02:08.027
He's been forging
since he was 10.

00:02:08.127 --> 00:02:11.063
But here we have
a twist-pattern Damascus.

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:13.065
What are we talking about here
with this?

00:02:13.199 --> 00:02:15.101
[Ben]<i> What Colton's gonna
 have to do is</i>

00:02:15.167 --> 00:02:16.902
<i>forge-weld his layers together,</i>

00:02:17.002 --> 00:02:18.237
<i> draw them out into a bar,</i>

00:02:18.337 --> 00:02:20.340
<i> and physically twist the bar.</i>

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:23.042
[Jarrett]<i> I'm going to get
 two billets forged out</i>

00:02:23.076 --> 00:02:24.477
<i> so I have twice
 as much material</i>

00:02:24.577 --> 00:02:27.046
to get those layers up
so you can see that pattern

00:02:27.146 --> 00:02:28.281
<i> and go from there.</i>

00:02:29.515 --> 00:02:31.950
[Wil] Colton, he's over there
on Big Blue

00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:33.920
and his forge welds look great.

00:02:34.053 --> 00:02:36.054
[Colton]<i> My welds
 are taking amazingly.</i>

00:02:36.188 --> 00:02:38.090
I'm not having
any problems at all.

00:02:38.191 --> 00:02:39.559
It's miraculous.

00:02:42.595 --> 00:02:45.431
-That stack is big and long.
-Yeah.

00:02:45.464 --> 00:02:48.700
[Scott]<i> I believe what's left
 has a very good weld on it.</i>

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:49.903
So I keep going.

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:54.641
I got the billet all drawn out.

00:02:56.809 --> 00:02:58.243
I wanna get the layer count up.

00:02:58.343 --> 00:03:00.312
So both of these are 16 layers,

00:03:00.446 --> 00:03:02.181
so that would be,
what, 32 layers.

00:03:02.314 --> 00:03:03.416
If the layers weren't there,

00:03:03.516 --> 00:03:05.618
that pattern would
kind of be muddled, so...

00:03:06.219 --> 00:03:08.922
I'm a little nervous
just because there's a big gap

00:03:09.022 --> 00:03:11.323
<i> in between these
 two pieces of steel.</i>

00:03:11.557 --> 00:03:14.160
But I fluxed it pretty good,
so hopefully it'll stick.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.261
[Colton]<i> I'm feeling pretty good
 right now.</i>

00:03:16.328 --> 00:03:18.464
I think I'm ready to twist.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:23.069
Right now I still
have 16 layers.

00:03:23.669 --> 00:03:25.638
[Wil] Scott still has
to do a restack.

00:03:25.738 --> 00:03:28.074
Yeah. Hopefully
he's got that math right.

00:03:28.474 --> 00:03:31.878
With seven layers of 16,
that'll give me over 100 layers.

00:03:32.645 --> 00:03:33.613
[exclaims]

00:03:35.214 --> 00:03:37.116
Man, that Colton,
he's doing a great job.

00:03:37.216 --> 00:03:39.384
And that's the cool thing.
Colton is done.

00:03:39.518 --> 00:03:41.254
All he has to do now
is make a knife.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:44.156
This kid is smart.

00:03:44.223 --> 00:03:46.125
[Colton]<i> I got an hour left
 to quench.</i>

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:48.628
And might as well
just do it now.

00:03:50.863 --> 00:03:51.964
Colton quenches.

00:03:52.865 --> 00:03:56.902
I pulled it out of the oil
and it's perfectly straight.

00:03:57.636 --> 00:04:00.673
Now I have plenty of time
to finish grinding the bevels.

00:04:01.507 --> 00:04:03.709
And there he goes,
Jarrett's in the quench.

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:05.544
[Jarrett]<i> Comes out
 fairly straight.</i>

00:04:05.711 --> 00:04:08.848
It has a little bit of a, uh,
corkscrew to it,

00:04:08.915 --> 00:04:11.450
<i> but nothing I wouldn't
 be able to grind out.</i>

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:13.219
I get the blade roughly shaped.

00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:15.221
<i> I'm pleased with the pattern.</i>

00:04:15.321 --> 00:04:16.756
I am not pleased
with the handle.

00:04:16.789 --> 00:04:19.358
<i> I'm going to have to do
 a lot of alterations to it</i>

00:04:19.492 --> 00:04:21.261
to make it work properly.

00:04:23.562 --> 00:04:25.465
Scott just quenched his blade.

00:04:26.765 --> 00:04:27.366
Whoa!

00:04:27.867 --> 00:04:29.835
[Scott]<i> Surprised
 it's pretty straight.</i>

00:04:29.869 --> 00:04:32.971
<i> But right now I've gotta
 get the scale off</i>

00:04:33.071 --> 00:04:34.339
<i> so I can dip it in acid</i>

00:04:34.406 --> 00:04:36.108
<i> before the clock runs out.</i>

00:04:36.175 --> 00:04:39.012
Three, two, one!

00:04:39.078 --> 00:04:40.713
Bladesmiths, shut down
your machines.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:41.647
Drop your tools.

00:04:41.747 --> 00:04:44.784
This first round
of Damascus competition

00:04:44.917 --> 00:04:45.985
is over.

00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:48.688
Bladesmiths, welcome
to the stone chop.

00:04:50.156 --> 00:04:51.690
In this test,
we're gonna test your edge

00:04:51.757 --> 00:04:53.760
and your overall construction
of your blades

00:04:53.893 --> 00:04:55.094
to see how they hold up.

00:04:55.161 --> 00:04:57.730
I'll be smashing them
into these stones.

00:04:57.764 --> 00:04:59.799
Colton, you're up first.
You ready?

00:04:59.899 --> 00:05:01.567
[rock music playing]

00:05:16.215 --> 00:05:17.516
Well, Colton...

00:05:17.616 --> 00:05:20.385
First things first,
your blade held up really well.

00:05:20.452 --> 00:05:23.589
There's evidence
of some serious scratching

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:24.757
from the stone,

00:05:24.824 --> 00:05:27.693
but we're bashing
a knife into a rock,

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:29.995
so I expect
some kind of abrasion,

00:05:30.095 --> 00:05:31.531
and that's what I find.

00:05:32.098 --> 00:05:35.501
This is an extremely
slick handle.

00:05:35.534 --> 00:05:37.503
There's not really much
on this handle

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:39.338
keeping me
from falling back here.

00:05:39.372 --> 00:05:41.373
But, all that being said,

00:05:41.407 --> 00:05:43.042
you got
a nice-looking knife here,

00:05:43.109 --> 00:05:44.344
so well done.

00:05:46.512 --> 00:05:48.180
Scott, how you feeling?

00:05:48.948 --> 00:05:51.551
-A little nervous.
-[laughs] Understandable.

00:05:54.954 --> 00:05:56.889
[rock music playing]

00:06:12.805 --> 00:06:15.308
Well, Scott, as you can see,
you're all in one piece.

00:06:15.375 --> 00:06:18.544
Um, this one took the slightest
of roll right here

00:06:18.677 --> 00:06:20.412
where I can feel it
underneath my finger.

00:06:20.545 --> 00:06:23.783
There's some cosmetic issues
going on here.

00:06:23.883 --> 00:06:25.617
Your spine jumps down,

00:06:25.718 --> 00:06:28.587
and then, handle jumps up.
And there's some gapping

00:06:28.687 --> 00:06:31.057
in between the guard
and the blade.

00:06:31.157 --> 00:06:32.658
But, uh, your blade
held up well.

00:06:32.692 --> 00:06:34.594
-Nice job.
-Thank you.

00:06:35.594 --> 00:06:38.497
-Jarrett, you're up. You ready?
-Sure.

00:06:38.597 --> 00:06:41.333
-All right. You sound excited.
-Yeah. [laughs]

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:43.669
[rock music playing]

00:06:59.251 --> 00:07:00.953
[exhales]

00:07:02.821 --> 00:07:05.625
Well, Jarrett, your, uh,
your blade held up really well.

00:07:06.725 --> 00:07:08.260
Your edge, where I was hitting,

00:07:08.327 --> 00:07:10.196
it's no longer
as sharp as it was.

00:07:10.729 --> 00:07:12.365
It's still got an edge in spots

00:07:12.498 --> 00:07:14.700
but there's a lot
of glinting along there.

00:07:16.035 --> 00:07:18.237
I really like the profile
you got going on here.

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:21.240
This reminds me
of old Celtic iron knives

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:22.341
and fun stuff like that.

00:07:22.774 --> 00:07:24.377
I like the proportions
of your handle.

00:07:24.444 --> 00:07:26.645
Put my hand in there,
everything is comfortable.

00:07:26.678 --> 00:07:29.381
All in all, it's still straight,
still good.

00:07:29.481 --> 00:07:32.185
-So, well done.
-Thank you.

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:36.923
[Doug] All right, bladesmiths.

00:07:37.056 --> 00:07:38.390
This is the sharpness test.

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:41.093
The leather bag slice.

00:07:41.227 --> 00:07:43.495
Now, this is all about
how sharp your blades are

00:07:43.529 --> 00:07:45.364
and how well
they cut these bags.

00:07:45.497 --> 00:07:47.600
Colton, you're up first.
You ready for this?

00:07:48.701 --> 00:07:50.002
Okay.

00:07:57.476 --> 00:07:59.011
[rock music playing]

00:08:06.586 --> 00:08:08.888
All right, Colton,
let's talk about your edge here.

00:08:08.988 --> 00:08:10.556
On the first two cuts,

00:08:10.689 --> 00:08:12.557
you can see right here,
it's not as sharp

00:08:12.624 --> 00:08:14.927
from the dulling
from cutting on the stone.

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:17.263
But the edge over here
is razor sharp

00:08:17.296 --> 00:08:20.132
and that part
did cut into this bag.

00:08:20.232 --> 00:08:22.902
So, your blade, it will cut.

00:08:25.771 --> 00:08:27.506
All right, Scott.
Your turn. Ready, sir?

00:08:27.639 --> 00:08:29.775
-Yes, sir.
-Let's do this.

00:08:30.609 --> 00:08:32.778
[rock music playing]

00:08:45.190 --> 00:08:48.093
All right, Scott. Some dulling
from hitting the stone.

00:08:48.193 --> 00:08:51.530
But there is some edge over here
that does allow for a cut

00:08:51.564 --> 00:08:52.697
on the bottom right there.

00:08:52.764 --> 00:08:55.467
-So, it will cut.
-Thank you.

00:08:55.534 --> 00:08:57.169
All right, Jarrett, your turn.
You ready?

00:08:57.269 --> 00:08:59.672
-Go for it.
-All right.

00:08:59.772 --> 00:09:03.276
[dramatic music playing]

00:09:18.557 --> 00:09:19.424
All right, Jarrett.

00:09:19.558 --> 00:09:21.626
The grind that you have
over here

00:09:21.726 --> 00:09:23.495
that was affected by the stone,

00:09:23.629 --> 00:09:26.566
it's not as sharp
to cut through these bags.

00:09:27.266 --> 00:09:28.234
It will not cut.

00:09:32.838 --> 00:09:34.606
Jarrett, it's pretty obvious

00:09:34.706 --> 00:09:37.176
your blade doesn't
make the cut, literally.

00:09:37.276 --> 00:09:38.344
-[chuckles]
-For that reason,

00:09:38.444 --> 00:09:40.312
I'd like to invite you
to shake our hands

00:09:40.412 --> 00:09:43.148
and then please exit the forge.
Come on forward, my friend.

00:09:43.249 --> 00:09:44.950
[Jarrett]<i> I think if I had
 a little more time</i>

00:09:45.084 --> 00:09:47.119
with not dealing with that crack
that came in there,

00:09:47.253 --> 00:09:50.155
would've had a little more time
to get that edge nice and sharp

00:09:50.322 --> 00:09:51.924
<i> and cut the bag.</i>

00:09:52.657 --> 00:09:54.092
<i> I had a blast competing</i>

00:09:54.226 --> 00:09:56.962
and everybody was super great,
so I had a fun time.

00:09:57.029 --> 00:09:59.732
It was cool
just for the experience.

